      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1914, thirty miles of the Port Bolivar Iron Ore Railway connected Ore City to Longview, but construction halted during World War I.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/ore-city-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "dropped_surname_no_wiki_source"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ore City sits where U.S. Highway 259 and Farm‑to‑Market Road 450 meet, just four miles south of Lake O’ the Pines.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ore_City%2C_Texas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first settlers arrived in the early 1850s from Haywood County, Tennessee, as part of the William Murray League.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ore_City%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Four miles north of Ore City lies Lake O' the Pines, a popular spot for fishing and boating in the Piney Woods region.",
